22:18:33 | INFO | 🤖 Target: The AI wasn’t saying your friend is “making it up.” It was taking seriously their report that people make assumptions about them personally. The broader claim is also too simplistic: - **“Persian countries” isn’t really a standard category.** Persian refers to an ethnic/cultural/linguistic identity. Iran is the main Persian-speaking country, but Persian people also live across many countries and in diaspora communities. - **Educational systems vary enormously within every country.** Access, school funding, language, family circumstances, universities, and individual achievement matter far more than nationality alone. - **Country-level comparisons do not justify judging an individual’s intelligence or education.** Even if one education system performs differently on some international measures, that does not tell you whether a particular Persian/Iranian person is well educated. - Iran, for example, has significant higher education and strong traditions in areas such as mathematics, engineering, medicine, literature, and science, while also having real inequalities and challenges—like virtually every country. So it is fair to discuss differences between education systems using reliable evidence and specific measures. It is not fair to turn those broad comparisons into a presumption that a Persian person is less educated. That presumption is likely what your friend is reacting to.
