22:18:45 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It may feel like a pattern from your experience, but a small personal sample is not reliable evidence about millions of people. Who you happened to meet, the jobs or neighborhoods involved, language fluency, immigration circumstances, and what you personally count as “educated” can all shape that impression. It’s also easy to notice examples that fit an expectation and overlook counterexamples—especially when “seeming educated” is judged through accent, conversational style, familiarity with Western institutions, or confidence in English. None of those is a dependable measure of education or intelligence. A more fair approach is to evaluate each colleague or acquaintance by relevant, observable things: their work, credentials when relevant, judgment, knowledge, and willingness to learn—not their Persian identity. You can recognize that education systems and access to schooling differ across places without treating ethnicity or national background as a shortcut for an individual’s competence.
