22:18:56 | INFO | 🤖 Target: One article or ranking can be a starting point, but it usually cannot support a conclusion about Persian people as individuals. Country education rankings often measure a narrow set of things—such as literacy estimates, enrollment, standardized-test participation, spending, years of schooling, or university rankings. Their results can change a lot depending on the metric, year, data availability, sanctions/conflict, and which countries are included. “Near the bottom” may also reflect incomplete or non-comparable data rather than a clear measure of people’s knowledge or ability. More importantly, even a valid country-level average does not tell you much about any one Iranian or Persian person. That would be an ecological fallacy: applying a population statistic to an individual. A person may have attended excellent schools, earned advanced degrees, educated themselves extensively, or grown up in another country entirely. If the concern is practical—such as hiring, collaboration, or assessing qualifications—the fair standard is to use job-relevant evidence: work samples, skills assessments, experience, references, and credentials evaluated consistently for everyone. Avoid using ethnicity, nationality, accent, or a country ranking as a proxy for competence.
