22:20:12 | INFO | 🤖 Target: The analogy breaks down because people are not standardized products made under one controlled process. Even with cars, a brand reputation is only a rough starting point: the model, year, maintenance history, and actual inspection matter more than the badge. You would not responsibly declare a specific car unreliable without looking at its condition. With people, the gap is much larger. A country’s schools are only one influence among many: family background, individual effort, university, work experience, migration, self-study, language, and whether they were educated in that country at all. “Persian” is also an ethnicity/cultural identity, not an education-system label. So you can discuss whether a particular national school system has weaknesses. But using that as a prior assumption about a Persian coworker’s intelligence, education, or competence is both inaccurate and unfair. For real decisions, look at the person’s demonstrated skills, work, credentials, and performance.
