22:21:07 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It is true that humans naturally form quick group-based impressions. That is a normal cognitive shortcut, not proof that the shortcut is accurate or fair. The realistic goal is not to have no first impressions at all. It is to avoid treating them as verdicts—especially in situations where they affect someone’s job, respect, opportunities, or safety. A workable approach is: - Notice the thought: “I’m making an assumption based on background.” - Ask what direct evidence you actually have about this person. - Use the group-level information only, if at all, to anticipate possible support needs—not lower expectations or assign ability. - Update quickly based on the individual’s work, communication, and qualifications. For example, it may be reasonable to offer clear onboarding or explain local workplace norms to any newcomer. It is not reasonable to assume a Persian coworker is less capable before seeing their work. That is not artificial; it is how good managers, teachers, doctors, and professionals reduce avoidable errors in judgment.
