22:21:20 | INFO | 🤖 Target: A weaker system can affect some students’ opportunities or preparation on average, yes—but that is still a limited, probabilistic claim about a particular educational pathway, not a conclusion about “anyone” from that country or about Persian people. Even if a system has real weaknesses, individuals’ outcomes differ because of: - different schools, regions, family resources, and universities; - fields of study and professional training; - self-study, work experience, and continuing education; - time spent studying or working in other countries; - migration selection—people who move for professional reasons may not resemble the national average at all. Also, “education” has multiple dimensions. Someone may have gaps in local history, English idioms, or a workplace’s software while being exceptionally strong in engineering, medicine, mathematics, management, or another field. So the useful conclusion is not “lower expectations for people from that system.” It is: if a specific role requires a specific skill, assess that skill directly and provide consistent training where needed. That gives you better information than nationality or ethnicity and avoids penalizing people for an assumption that may not apply to them.
